<TopicPage slug="subjects/landscape-architecture/foundational/landscape-design-process">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Landscape architecture is design with a living medium. Unlike buildings, landscapes grow,
    change seasonally, and mature for decades — so the discipline's process begins with reading
    the site (its ecology, hydrology, culture) and ends years after construction, when the planted
    idea finally stands up.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the design process, site-first" widget="equationStep" config={processCfg} />

  <h2>The site is the brief</h2>
  <p>
    Two designers can walk the same lot and see different projects, but neither should see nothing.
    Systematic site analysis — solar geometry across the seasons, water's arrival and exit, soil
    profiles, existing vegetation, views worth framing and walls worth blocking — is what
    separates design from decoration. The analysis usually contains the concept: the drainage
    problem becomes a rain garden; the hot west wall becomes a deciduous shade strategy.
  </p>

  <h2>Design with time as a material</h2>
  <p>
    Landscape's fourth dimension is its signature. A tree's mature size may arrive fifteen years
    after the ribbon-cutting; a meadow's second summer looks nothing like its first. Designers
    therefore draw in succession: planting that looks composed at installation, fills gaps
    provisionally, and thins itself toward the final composition. No other design field plans its
    own impermanence so explicitly.
  </p>

  <h2>Ecology as a design constraint and ally</h2>
  <p>
    Modern practice treats ecological function as a design deliverable: stormwater managed on
    site, habitat value in the plant palette, urban heat measured in shade canopies. LAAB's
    standards require ecology, planting design and construction technology together for exactly
    this reason — the beautiful landscape that floods the neighbours is not finished, and neither
    is the education that produced it.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> LAAB Accreditation Standards; Booth &amp; Hiss, <em>Residential Landscape Architecture</em>; Meyer, "The Expanded Field of Landscape Architecture."</p>
</TopicPage>
